STDIN=      5
STDOUT=     6
STDTAB=     7
UNITS=  ENGLISH
NCMD=      27
FEQX        1
FLOODWAY    2
BRIDGE      3
CULVERT     4
FINISH      5
SAME        6
FEQXLST     8
ROADFLOW    9
SEWER      10
MULPIPES   11
FTABIN     12
EMBANKQ    13
JUMP       14
CRITQ      15
GRITTER    16
MULCON     18
CHANRAT    19
EXPCON     20
HEC2X      21
QCLIMIT    22
XSINTERP   23
FEQXEXT    25
CHANNEL    26
WSPROX     27
WSPROQZ    28
WSPROT14   29
UFGATE     30
;     The lines above this one are part of what is called the standard
;     header.  The standard header defines the names for the commands.
;     Each of these commands has an internal number.  For example,
;     the CHANRAT command has internal number 19.  This number
;     does not change as new commands are added.  The name, `CHANRAT'
;     however, can be changed if you do not like the name.  Changing
;     the name in the standard header gives the command a new name.
;     Of course, you must then use the new name if you want FEQUTL
;     to find the command.
;
;     DZLIM gives the minimum depth increment in a table of cross sectional
;     elements.  The smaller is DZLIM the more precisely the conveyance
;     and the velocity distribution correction coefficients will be
;     interpolated from the table.
DZLIM= 2.5
;     NRZERO gives a near zero depth so that interpolation for conveyance
;     at small depths will have less error.
NRZERO= 0.08
;     USGSBETA should be NO in most cases.  See the input manual for FEQUTL.
USGSBETA=NO
;     EPSARG and EPSF are convergence criteria used in seeking a root.
;     EPSARG gives the difference between two successive approximations
;     to a root relative to the estimated root below which FEQUTL decides
;     that the root has been found.  When the absolute value of the function
;     defining the root is less than EPSF, FEQUTL also decides a root has
;     been found.
EPSARG=5.E-5
EPSF=1.E-4
;     EXTEND=NO means that the low side of a cross section will not be
;     extended to match the higher side.  EXTEND=YES means that the
;     low side of a cross section will be extended with a vertical
;     frictionless wall to match the higher side.
EXTEND=NO
 
        Example file for FEQUTL illustrating many of its features.
 
 
*       With version 3.51 and later of FEQUTL, you may place comments
*       with an asterisk in column one of the line at any point in the
*       input.  Comments with an asterisk will be written in the
*       output file.  A line beginning with a semi-colon in column
*       one is not written in the output file.  It is only seen in
*       the input.
 
        Old style comments, like this one, beginning on or after
        column nine may still be used but this style comment can only
        appear between commands but NEVER within the body of a command.
 
 
 
        Example of table in FEQX format illustrating the
        propagation of subsection numbers into blank fields.
 
FEQX
TABLE#=  153 NOOUT SAVE22
STATION=     1.070
NAVM=    0
NSUB    3     0.140     0.050     0.140
    OFFSET ELEVATION SUBS
    -750.     720.      1
    -400.     710.
*     Note that the missing subsection numbers will propagate from above
*     when using FEQX.  The numbers need only fall somewhere within the
*     assigned field width.  I prefer to place the numbers in columns
*     if I use FEQX to make checking input easier.  However, for my own
*     use I prefer FEQXLST because it allows me to type the numbers
*     more quickly.
    -100.     704.08
     -47.     704.08    2
     -20.     695.08
       0.0    695.08
      20.     695.08
      47.     704.08    3
     100.     704.08
*     The line segment ending at (100, 704.08) is horizontal but it is
*     not at the  minimum point in the cross section.  This represents a berm
*     in the cross section.  Such a horizontal line above the bottom of
*     the cross section is invalid in FEQUTL because it creates a
*     discontinuous function.  The top width at elevation 704.08 is
*     undefined.  In order to force a defined top width we put a small
*     transverse slope on the berm.  Clearly a strictly horizontal berm
*     is a figment of some designer's desire.  There will always be some
*     slope on the berm.  The increment, 0.053, was picked years ago to
*     be small, and an odd size so that such increments could be recognized
*     in the tables if needed.  You may give an increment of your choice
*     by changing the elevation in the input directly.
     300.     710.
     700.     719.     -1
 
 
 
        Example of cross section table in FEQXLST format.
        Shows example of checking that the offsets never
        decrease to help check for errors when keying in natural
        channel cross sections and also shows a request to save
        the resulting cross section table in the memory of FEQUTL
        so that we can refer to this table later.
 
 
 
FEQXLST
TABLE#=   18 MONOTONE SAVE20 NOOUT
*     A whole series of options can follow the table number.  These
*     options are read from left to right and the most recent option,
*     should any conflict, encountered is the one used.
STATION= 2.412
NAVM=0     SCALE=1.0
*     The scale factor above is a multiplier on the offsets.  It does not
*     affect the elevations.
NSUB    1     0.030
    OFFSET ELEVATION  SUB
;     FEQXLST requires that the subsection number be typed for each point
;     so that there are always 3 numbers on each line.  Only a single space
;     is required to separate the numbers on a line.  Commas may also be
;     used to separate the numbers on a line.
0.0  40.0  1
0.0 0.0   1
100.0 0.0   1
100.0 40.0 -1
 
        Shows example of multiple subsections.  Note that the subsection
        number is given at the start of the line segment because the
        last subsection number is used to terminate the input.
        That is, the line segment from offset 663 to 664 is in
        subsection 1.  The line segment from 659.2 to 661.2 is in
        subsection 2 and  the line segment from 661.2 to 665 is in
        subsection 3.  Finally the subsection value of -1 at offset
        674 signals the end of the cross section.
 
FEQXLST
TABLE#=    4  SAVE12  NEWBETA  OUT12
STATION= 2.095
NAVM=0     SCALE=50.0
*     The value of scale is used here to correct for map scale.  The
*     offsets are in inches from a topographic map and the scale factor
*     converts inches to feet on the ground.
NSUB    3     0.080     0.055     0.090
HEADING
0.0 674.0 1
0.6 670.0 1
2.3 664.0 1
4.0 663.8 1
5.4 664.0 1
5.7 666.0 1
6.4 667.0 1
7.2 667.3 1
7.8 667.0 1
8.5 666.0 1
9.0 666.1 1
10.8 668.0 1
11.6 670.0 1
12.2 670.1 1
13.0 668.0 1
15.0 668.1 1
16.0 667.0 1
25.6 667.1 1
25.7 666.0 1
28.8 665.0 1
32.5 665.1 1
33.2 664.0 1
33.6 663.0 1
33.8 664.0 2
34.7 658.6 2
35.1 657.8 2
35.8 658.1 2
36.1 658.6 2
36.4 659.2 2
36.9 661.2 3
38.3 665.0 3
38.4 666.0 3
39.2 667.0 3
39.8 673.5 -1
 
 
        Shows use of scale option to adjust the horizontal scale
        of the cross section.
        Also shows how we can ask to save the cross section without
        requesting monotonicity checking and at the same time suppress
        output to the table file.   We have added additional points
        to improve the GRITTER computations.
 
FEQXLST
TABLE#=   19 SAVE NOOUT
STATION= 2.412
NAVM=0     SCALE=0.8
NSUB    1     0.030
    OFFSET ELEVATION  SUB
0.0  20.0  1
0.0  19.0  1
0.0  18.0  1
0.0  17.0  1
0.0  16.0  1
0.0  15.0 1
0.0  14.0 1
0.0  13.0 1
0.0  12.0 1
0.0  11.0 1
0.0  10.0 1
0.0  9.0 1
0.0  8.0 1
0.0  7.0 1
0.0  6.0 1
0.0  5.0 1
;0.0  4.0 1
;0.0  3.0 1
;0.0  2.0 1
;0.0 1.0 1
;0.0 0.0   1
100.0 5.0   1
100.0 20.0 -1
 
        Example of the FEQXEXT command.  It permits optional
        variation of Manning's n in the vertical in each subsection.
        In the example shown here subsections 1 and 2 have request
        such variation.  The * in the column headed N0 selects
        the n value given for the subsection on the line labeled
        with NSUB as the roughness to use at zero depth in the
        subsection.  Thus in subsection 1 the Manning's n is
        0.140 at zero depth and varies linearly with water
        depth until a depth is 1.0.  Above that level the
        n value is constant.  The choice of depth is given
        on the VARN line.  MAXY uses the maximum depth in the
        subsection as the variable to which variation of n is
        related.  Another options in HYDY, hydraulic depth in
        the subsection.   Comments for each boundary point
        can be given but they must be prefixed by a single
        quote.
 
        Subsection 3 shows an example of having the n
        value on the boundary vary from one line segment
        to the next.  FEQXEXT computes a perimeter-weighted
        value of Manning's n in this case.  The * is needed
        to signal that the subsection number is to take
        the default value.  The default for a subsection number is
        always the most recent subsection number encountered.
 
 
 
FEQXEXT
TABID=   154 OUT22 OLDBETA
STATION=     1.25
VARN=MAXY
NSUB    3      .140      .050      .140
    OFFSET ELEVATION SUBS    N0   Y1    N1   Y2  N2  Y3  N3  Y4  N4
    -750.     720.      1     *  1.0   .08
    -400.     710.
    -130.     704.1
     -47.     704.5     2     *   4.0   0.04  'Banks are smoother.
     -20.     695.
       0.0    694.
      20.     695.
      47.     704.1     3   0.06
     130.     704.5     *    0.10
     300.     710.      *    0.15
     700.     719.     -1
 
        Example of computing a critical flow table using the just
        computed cross section tables.  We also request saving of
        the critical flow table so we can use it later in the input.
 
 
CRITQ
TABID=    50 SAVE
APPTAB#=   18
CONTAB#=   19
DISCOEF=  0.98
TEST OF CRITICAL FLOW TABLE
 
        Example of use of the just computed critical flow table
        for the generalized Ritter solution to the classical
        dam break problem.
 
GRITTER
APPTAB#=   18
CONTAB#=   50
;BRCHHGT=   0.0
     DEPTH DISCHARGE   TEST OF GRITTER SOLUTION
       15.       0.0
       15.     100.0
       15.     200.0
       15.0    500.0
      -1.0
 
 
