        Example of interpolating for cross section tables that
        will be output and that will be used in FEQ
        All of the tables that are used to interpolate from
        must have already been created and saved in this run
        or they must have been input using FTABIN
 
XSINTERP
SFAC= 5280.
NODEID=YES
 NODE NODEID  XNUM   STATION ELEVATION   KA   KD
   31          153     1.992  657.75
;      A negative table number tells FEQUTL that you want to interpolate
;      a table.  If you leave station and elevation blank FEQUTL will
;      divide the distance into equal parts and will interpolate
;      linearly for the elevation.
              -810
                 4     2.089  657.74
              -812
;              FEQUTL will use the station and elevation given here as
;              being measured.  Thus the elevation at cross section
;              18 will be interpolated from this value also.
              -813     2.300  657.00
              9000     2.398
;       If you use only a - in the right-most column of the field for
;       the cross section table number, then FEQUTL will assign the
;       next available number based on the tables it is currently
;       retaining in memory.  This could conflict with a table number
;       that you will input later!
                 -
              -817
                 -
                19     2.862  656.96
   -1
 
 
 
 
*     Example of the command to process HEC2 input files and extract
*     the basic, unmodified cross sections as defined by the
*     NC, NH, X1, X4, and GR cards.  No other cards are recognized by this
*     initial version of HEC2X.  Over time, the command may be
*     generalized to recognize some of the modification cards that
*     HEC2 uses.
 
 
HEC2X
;     There are two modes: DIRECT and INDIRECT.  In the DIRECT mode
;     FEQUTL computes a cross section table from the HEC2 cross section.
;     In the INDIRECT mode, FEQUTL computes the input for the FEQX
;     command and places the cross sections in a file.  These cross
;     sections can then be modified before computing the cross section
;     tables in another run of FEQUTL.
MODE=DIRECT
;     INFILE is where you give the name of the file containing the
;     HEC2 format input.  The entire input to HEC2 can exist in the
;     file and HEC2X will search and find the cards it needs.
INFILE=../data/model01.inp
;     OUTFILE gives the name of the file used for the output of the
;     FEQX format cross sections when MODE=INDIRECT.  OUTFILE must
;     still appear if MODE=DIRECT but the name can be left blank.
OUTFILE=HECFEQX
;     OPTIONS gives the save, output, extend, and checking options to use
;     on each cross section in the file given in INFILE.  These options
;     are the same as for FEQX.  These options are appended at the proper
;     location for each cross section written to OUTFILE when MODE=INDIRECT.
OPTIONS: OUT20 MONOTONE NEWBETA
;     Each table in FEQ and FEQUTL must have a number.  To assign numbers
;     to tables from the HEC2 input, BEGTAB gives the starting table number
;     followed by a table number increment. Five columns are allocated
;     for each number so watch the spacing.  The first valid cross section
;     found in the file given in INFILE is assigned the initial number.
;     The table number then has the increment added to it to yield the
;     next table number.  Thus the tables are numbered in the sequence
;     in which they are encountered in the HEC2 input.  Each cross section
;     in INFILE is considered to be a new cross section.
BEGTAB#= 1100  -2
;     BEGSTAT gives the beginning station for the first cross section.
;     Following the beginning station is the sign(+1 or -1) to use
;     in adding the inter-section distance along the channel.  Thus
;     you can select to have the stations increase or decrease as the
;     file named in INFILE is processed.  Note that 10 columns are
;     allocated to each number.   The number can appear anywhere in the
;     field so long as it is in the correct field.
BEGSTAT=00000000.0   1
;     SFAC gives the conversion factor from the units used in HEC2 for the
;     distance between cross sections to the units you want in the
;     stationing in your unsteady flow model.  SFAC is a divisor on the
;     distances used in HEC2.  If the distances in HEC2 are feet, as they
;     are in most cases, then SFAC=5280 will produce stations along the
;     the channel in miles.
SFAC= 5280.
 
 
 
;     Example of MUCON using a mud line and roughness in the pipes.
;     You must supply a mudline and roughness for each pipe if you
;     have mud in only one pipe.  The other pipes would have
;     a mud line of 0.0
;
 
MULCON
TABID=  -108      OUT22
WSLOT= 0.005
HSLOT= 24.0
NPIPES=    5
TYPE:      RCPA      NHE        NVE      CIRC        TE
SPAN:                         12.00     12.00      3.15
RISE:      2.00     12.00                2.00     12.00
BOTT:       1.0       2.0       0.0       0.4       0.8
ROUG:      0.02      0.02      0.02      0.02      0.02
MUDL:      0.25      0.5       2.0       0.2       5.0
ROUG:     0.028     0.029     0.030     0.031     0.032
 
 
;     A mud line can be given in MULPIPES and SEWER also.
;     This is an example of two of the pipes not having
;     mud but they must still have a non-zero roughness
;     value and a zero value for MUDL.
 
MULPIPES
TABID=  -651 OUT22
NSIDES=  50  0.02
WSLOT= 0.01
HSLOT= 5.0
NPIPES=    3
DIAM=       3.5       4.0       3.5
BOTT=       0.5       0.0       0.5
ROUG=     0.020     0.020     0.020
MUDL=       4.0       0.0       0.0
ROUG=     0.025     0.035     0.028
 
 
 
SEWER
TABID=   637 OUT22
DIAMETER= 3.0
NSIDES=  30
WSLOT= 0.001
HSLOT=5.0
N= 0.020
MUDL= 1.05
MUDN=0.03
 
 
 
;     FEQUTL can generate a CHANNEL command from HEC-2 input stream.
;     Mode must be channel and it will always create an outfile.
;     If you omit the name of the OUTFILE, HEC2X will store the
;     channel command in a file named: CHANNEL.  To complete
;     processing you must copy the contents of the OUTFILE, after
;     you have checked it for validity, into an input stream for
;     FEQUTL.  An example is given below this example.
 
 
HEC2X
MODE=CHANNEL
INFILE=../data/model01.inp
OUTFILE=utlexm.chx
OPTIONS: OUT22  NEWBETA
BEGTAB#= 1200  -2
BEGSTAT=00000000.0   1
SFAC=5280.
 
 
;     Here is an example of a CHANNEL command computed from a HEC-2
;     input stream using HEC2X.  In this example we have three flow
;     lines: one on the left overbank, one on the distance axis,
;     and one on the right overbank.  FEQUTL assumes that you
;     want to treat the sinuosity as piecewise constant because
;     only two offsets appear and there are three flow lines.
;     FEQUTL will compute the sinuosity from the stationing
;     using a cubic spline fit.  No end conditions are given
;     so FEQUTL will use the assumption that the second derivative
;     of the flowline distance with respect to the axis distance
;     is zero at each end.
 
 
 
 
CHANNEL
SINDEF=CUBIC
HEAD      LOB                AXIS                 ROB
 
STAT 0.0000000           0.0000000           0.0000000
OFFS               75.00              153.00
 
STAT 0.0606061           0.0700758           0.0833333
OFFS              138.30              164.20
 
STAT 0.1496212           0.1553030           0.1666667
OFFS               92.70              168.70
 
STAT 0.1560606           0.1617424           0.1731061
OFFS              103.40              236.40
 
STAT 0.1655303           0.1712121           0.1825758
OFFS              142.80              210.80
 
STAT 0.3037879           0.3170455           0.3303030
OFFS               93.90              109.50
 
STAT 0.3918561           0.4051136           0.4183712
OFFS               93.90              109.50
 
STAT 0.4827651           0.4931818           0.5035985
OFFS               10.00               21.01
 
STAT 0.5198864           0.5303030           0.5407197
OFFS               10.00               21.01
 
STAT 0.5350379           0.5492424           0.5634470
OFFS              158.00              183.50
 
STAT 0.5558712           0.5691288           0.5823864
OFFS              138.90              178.10
 
STAT 0.5643939           0.5776515           0.5909091
OFFS              112.50              146.20
 
STAT 0.5729167           0.5861742           0.5994318
OFFS              108.90              167.80
 
STAT 0.6164773           0.6278409           0.6392046
OFFS               91.30              119.60
 
STAT 0.6505682           0.6657197           0.6827651
OFFS               41.00               64.80
END
 
